% The Ponens policies cert-secure-coding, as a program of Logical English for LPS.
%
% Written by lpsPlus's ponens_twin.pl (docs/migration/ponens.md) from 11
% Ponens policies, whose files are in sources/ (Ponens: github.com/imandra-ai/
% ponens, MIT licence, Imandra Inc.), with 2 Ponens traces as its scenarios.
%
% Each step of a trace is an action of the session Ponens recorded. Each
% policy is a causal law that records a breach: at the step where the policy
% fails, when the step ends, or at the end of the session when the policy
% looks forward. Where a scenario has a comment above it, the comment is what
% Ponens's own evaluator (`ponens trace check --json`) says of the same
% policies on that trace.
%
% DISCLAIMER. This program was written by a translator from the policies of
% another system. It is provided "as is", without warranty of any kind,
% express or implied, including any warranty that it is accurate, complete or
% fit for a particular purpose. The translation may be wrong: check it against
% its source before relying on it. It is not legal, regulatory or other
% professional advice. Its authors accept no liability for any loss or damage
% arising from its use.

the target language is: lps.

the maximum time is 20.

the events are:
    step *a step* is a *a kind*; known as step_is_a.
    step *a step* shows *a sign*; known as step_shows.
    step *a step* ends; known as step_ends.
    the trace shows *a sign*; known as trace_shows.
    the session ends; known as session_ends.

the fluents are:
    step *a step* was a *a kind*; known as was_a.
    step *a step* has shown *a sign*; known as has_shown.
    step *a step* has ended; known as has_ended.
    the trace has shown *a sign*; known as trace_has_shown.
    step *a step* meets *a condition*; known as meets.
    the policy *a policy* is broken at step *a step*; known as broken_at.

the knowledge base cert secure coding includes:

% What a trace records, kept to the end of the session.
when step a step is a a kind
then step the step was a the kind.

when step a step shows a sign
then step the step has shown the sign.

when step a step ends
then step the step has ended.

when the trace shows a sign
then the trace has shown the sign.

% cert_conformance_recorded (error): G(Release → P(conformance_summary))
% SEI CERT conformance: a project records its conformance to the ruleset — the guidelines checked, the findings and their levels, and any deviations.
step a step meets "an earlier step shows conformance summary" at a time if
    step a second step has ended at the time
    and the second step < the step
    and step the second step has shown "conformance summary" at the time.

when step a step ends
    and step the step was a Release
    and it is not the case that step the step meets "an earlier step shows conformance summary"
then the policy cert_conformance_recorded is broken at step the step.

% cert_deviation_documented (error): G(Deviation → rationale_recorded ∧ deviation_approved)
% SEI CERT: a documented deviation records the rule, the rationale (e.g. a false positive or an acceptable risk), and the approval allowing the violation to remain.
step a step meets "shows rationale recorded and shows deviation approved" at a time if
    step the step has shown "rationale recorded" at the time
    and step the step has shown "deviation approved" at the time.

when step a step ends
    and step the step was a Deviation
    and it is not the case that step the step meets "shows rationale recorded and shows deviation approved"
then the policy cert_deviation_documented is broken at step the step.

% cert_l1_remediated (error): G(level_l1 → remediated)
% SEI CERT: L1 findings (high severity, likely, low remediation cost — Priority 12–27) are addressed first; they represent the most urgent risks.
when step a step ends
    and step the step has shown "level l1"
    and it is not the case that step the step has shown "remediated"
then the policy cert_l1_remediated is broken at step the step.

% cert_l2_reviewed (warning): G(level_l2 → reviewed)
% SEI CERT: L2 findings (Priority 6–9) are addressed after L1; they should be reviewed and dispositioned.
when step a step ends
    and step the step has shown "level l2"
    and it is not the case that step the step has shown "reviewed"
then the policy cert_l2_reviewed is broken at step the step.

% cert_no_open_l1_at_release (error): G(Release → ¬open_l1)
% SEI CERT prioritization: L1 risks are remediated before lower-priority work and must not ship; an open L1 at release is a critical security gap.
when step a step ends
    and step the step was a Release
    and step the step has shown "open l1"
then the policy cert_no_open_l1_at_release is broken at step the step.

% cert_priority_assigned (error): G(Finding → priority_assigned)
% SEI CERT: the three risk factors multiply to a Priority (1–27), which maps to a Level — L1 (Priority 12–27), L2 (6–9), L3 (1–4).
when step a step ends
    and step the step was a Finding
    and it is not the case that step the step has shown "priority assigned"
then the policy cert_priority_assigned is broken at step the step.

% cert_recommendation_departure_recorded (warning): G(Finding ∧ cert_recommendation_departure → recorded)
% SEI CERT: Recommendations are advisory guidelines that improve security; departures are permissible but should be recorded.
when step a step ends
    and step the step was a Finding
    and step the step has shown "cert recommendation departure"
    and it is not the case that step the step has shown "recorded"
then the policy cert_recommendation_departure_recorded is broken at step the step.

% cert_risk_assessed (error): G(Finding → severity_assessed ∧ likelihood_assessed ∧ remediation_assessed)
% SEI CERT risk assessment: each guideline/finding is scored on Severity (low/medium/high), Likelihood (unlikely/probable/likely), and Remediation Cost (high/medium/low).
step a step meets "shows severity assessed and shows likelihood assessed and shows remediation assessed" at a time if
    step the step has shown "severity assessed" at the time
    and step the step has shown "likelihood assessed" at the time
    and step the step has shown "remediation assessed" at the time.

when step a step ends
    and step the step was a Finding
    and it is not the case that step the step meets "shows severity assessed and shows likelihood assessed and shows remediation assessed"
then the policy cert_risk_assessed is broken at step the step.

% cert_rule_violation_remediated (error): G(Finding ∧ cert_rule_violation → remediated ∨ deviation_approved)
% SEI CERT: Rules are normative requirements — a violation is a defect that must be corrected; an exception is permissible only via a documented, approved deviation.
step a step meets "shows remediated or shows deviation approved" at a time if
    step the step has shown "remediated" at the time.

step a step meets "shows remediated or shows deviation approved" at a time if
    step the step has shown "deviation approved" at the time.

when step a step ends
    and step the step was a Finding
    and step the step has shown "cert rule violation"
    and it is not the case that step the step meets "shows remediated or shows deviation approved"
then the policy cert_rule_violation_remediated is broken at step the step.

% cert_static_analysis_before_commit (error): G(GitCommit → P(StaticAnalysis ∧ cert_ruleset))
% SEI CERT conformance: an analyzer diagnoses rule violations; conformance is established by analysis (plus manual review for undecidable rules) before code is accepted.
step a step meets "an earlier step is a StaticAnalysis and shows cert ruleset" at a time if
    step a second step has ended at the time
    and the second step < the step
    and step the second step was a StaticAnalysis at the time
    and step the second step has shown "cert ruleset" at the time.

when step a step ends
    and step the step was a GitCommit
    and it is not the case that step the step meets "an earlier step is a StaticAnalysis and shows cert ruleset"
then the policy cert_static_analysis_before_commit is broken at step the step.

% cert_undecidable_manual_review (warning): G(undecidable_violation → manual_review)
% SEI CERT: some rules are undecidable; a sound analyzer over-approximates, and remaining cases require manual review to confirm conformance.
when step a step ends
    and step the step has shown "undecidable violation"
    and it is not the case that step the step has shown "manual review"
then the policy cert_undecidable_manual_review is broken at step the step.

